    {
      "id": "sd_1",
      "entity_type": "study_design",
      "data": {
        "id": "sd_1",
        "name": "Study Design",
        "instanceType": "InterventionalStudyDesign",
        "description": "Study GZBY is a Phase 3, multicenter, randomized, parallel-arm, double-blind, placebo- controlled study to investigate the efficacy and safety of retatrutide 4 mg, 9 mg, or 12 mg compared with placebo in participants with T2D and inadequate glycemic control with diet and exercise alone, are naïve to insulin use and have not been treated with any antihyperglycemic medication during the 90 days preceding Visit 1. Approximately 480 participants will be randomly assigned in a 1:1:1:1 ratio to retatrutide 4 mg (approximately 120), retatrutide 9 mg (approximately 120), retatrutide 12 mg (approximately 120), and placebo (approximately 120), respectively. This study will consist of 3 periods: • a screening period: up to 4 weeks • a treatment period: 40 weeks, including dose escalation and maintenance, and • a safety follow-up period: 4 weeks.",
        "rationale": "Choice of primary endpoint The primary endpoint of HbA1c is a commonly used endpoint in clinical trials evaluating the efficacy and safety of antihyperglycemic agents. Choice of comparator Regulatory authorities require the safety profile of new drugs to be well-characterized, including a sufficient number of placebo exposures. Per ICH E 1 (EMEA 1995), a placebo-controlled design is necessary to directly compare the AE rate in the drug-treated group with the background event rate in the patient population being studied. Double-blind design A double-blind design was selected to minimize participant and investigator bias in assessments for efficacy, safety, and study intervention tolerability. Duration of the study treatment The planned duration of the study treatment for 40 weeks is considered sufficient and appropriate for participants to optimize dosing of retatrutide and to assess the full effects and benefit/risk of each treatment dose of retatrutide on glycemic control. Collection of race and ethnicity data In this study, collection of demographic information includes race and ethnicity. The scientific rationale is based on the need to assess variable response in safety and/or efficacy based on race or ethnicity. This question can be answered only if all the relevant data are collected.",
        "model": {
